---
/**
 * /about/editorial-policy — the commitments that keep this site honest.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  description="Arthanomy's editorial commitments: independence from advertisers and issuers, no affiliate links, corrections in public, and the boundary between research and advice."
  path="/about/editorial-policy"
>
  <header class="shell pt-10">
    <p class="eyebrow">Editorial policy</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 max-w-3xl font-serif text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What this site owes its readers</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="shell article-body mt-8 max-w-3xl pb-16">
    <h2>Independence</h2>
    <p>
      Arthanomy accepts no advertising, sponsorship, placement fees or affiliate commissions. No broker, fund house,
      distributor, platform or issuer pays for coverage, favourable treatment or silence. There are no affiliate links
      on the site; where a link leaves Arthanomy it goes to the primary source or the official product page, not to
      a referral destination.
    </p>

    <h2>Funding</h2>
    <p>
      The site is funded by its readers through the voluntary support link in the footer
      (<a href={site.author.support} rel="noopener">Buy me a coffee</a>). Supporters receive no influence over content,
      no special coverage, and no advance access of any kind.
    </p>

    <h2>The advice boundary</h2>
    <p>
      The author is not a SEBI-registered investment adviser, research analyst or portfolio manager, and this site
      provides no personalised advice. Content is general research intended to inform the reader's own decisions or
      their conversations with a registered professional. Nothing here is an offer or solicitation to buy or sell any
      security.
    </p>

    <h2>Accuracy and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every factual claim carries a source; every tax or regulatory claim carries an effective date and a verification
      date. Errors, when found, are corrected on the page itself, with the change logged on the
      <a href={href('/about/corrections')}>corrections</a> page and the article's updated date revised. There is no
      quiet-edit policy: a figure that changes without notice is indistinguishable from a figure that was wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Product availability vs product history</h2>
    <p>
      Where a product existed historically but is currently closed, suspended or unavailable — small-savings tranches,
      sovereign gold bonds, discontinued schemes — the site distinguishes the history from current availability and
      does not require you to guess which it is describing.
    </p>

    <h2>High-return claims</h2>
    <p>
      Claims of returns above market norms are handled under the <a href={href('/research/high-return')}>four-lane
      framework</a>. A number is never presented without stating which lane it belongs to, and no blended “expected
      return” is manufactured by averaging quantities that are not comparable.
    </p>

    <h2>AI assistance</h2>
    <p>
      Drafting uses AI assistance under human direction. Every published piece is the responsibility of the named
      author, and verification statements are limited to what has actually been verified. This disclosure exists
      because a reader deciding how much to trust a page deserves to know how it was made — see the
      <a href={href('/about/methodology')}>methodology</a> for the full process.
    </p>

    <h2>Comments, email and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Correspondence is read and corrections are welcomed. Email addresses and identities of correspondents are never
      published without consent and never shared with third parties. The <a href={href('/privacy')}>privacy page</a>
      states exactly what the site does and does not collect. There are no comment sections to moderate because there
      are no comment sections.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
